          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have stage 3 kidney disease and high blood pressure. Does the classification link the two, and what target does guidance describe for someone like me?",
          "answer": "Yes, medical classification links stage 3 kidney disease and high blood pressure. In the ICD-10-CM system, a combination code (I12) is used when a person has both hypertension and chronic kidney disease, with a second code added for the specific stage, like stage 3 (coding-hypertension-i10). This indicates that a relationship between the two conditions is presumed (coding-hypertension-i10).\n\nGuidance describes a blood pressure target below 130/80 mm Hg for most people, including those with chronic kidney disease (Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis\"). When chronic kidney disease is present, a class of medications called renin-angiotensin blockade is preferred for blood pressure control (Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis\" and \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\"). Chronic kidney disease is a condition that shifts the choice of blood pressure medication (Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\").\n\nYou should discuss your blood pressure targets and treatment options with your doctor (Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\") or clinician (coding-hypertension-i10).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
